    {
      "id": "9Wav6JO7K2limVZnJqIHa",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3056.891653168412,
      "y": 493.558462,
      "width": 1227.319091796875,
      "height": 925,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b67",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1138703435,
      "version": 2791,
      "versionNonce": 263794795,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1727133175582,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Step 3. Deep dive (Try scaling each, identify the bottlenecks)\n\n1. Web servers: stateless servers, horizontal scale\n2. WebSocker servers: Stateful, consistent hashing\n    - before a server removal, drain existing connections\n    - by marking the server to be in 'draining' state on LB\n    - so that no new conn will be routed to it\n    - auto-scaling stateful server is the job of a good LB\n3. User DB: sharding by user id\n4. Location cache: needs to handle 10 million concurrent users\n    - can shard cache server by id to handle 340k QPS\n    - need replicate to have HA\n5. Redis Pub/Sub server: (Identified as BottleNeck)\n    - Calc the number of pub/sub servers\n    - 1B * 10% = 100M users, each has a channel\n    - 400 average friends per user, 100 of them online\n    - 100M * 100 * 20bytes = 200GB memory\n    - 10M concurrent users, each has 40 online friends, over 30 seconds\n    - 10M * 40 / 30 = 13.33M updates per second\n    - Assume each redis server can push 100K updates to subscribers, system need 133 Redis servers\n    - Shard by ID, service discovery by etcd or zookeeper, keep a list of servers in the service \n        discovery component, and a simple API to update it. service discovery is a small kv store\n        for holding configuration data; clients(WebSocket servers) subscribe to any updates to the value(Redis servers)\n    - consistency hashing using hash ring\n    - over-provisioned cluster\n    - deal with mass resubscription events, location updates might be missed by clients and should be minimized\n    - when a pub/sub server goes down, the monitoring software should alert the on-call operator\n    - subscribe/unsubscribe callback on client app.\n    - users with large number of friends could cause hotspot issues - put a hard cap say 5000, p/b servers \n            will be scattered among many WebSocket servers, unlikely to cause hotspots\n    - Nearby random person - similar design, but maintain p/s servers for the area with geohash instead of friend list\n    - Erlang(programming language) is a great alternative to Redis P/S server, but hiring good Erlang programmers is hard\n    - Elixir, Erlang virtual machine BEAM, Erlang runtime OTP\n6. Use cases\n    - Add/remove friends\n    - Hotspots issues\n    - Extension: nearby random person",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Step 3. Deep dive (Try scaling each, identify the bottlenecks)\n\n1. Web servers: stateless servers, horizontal scale\n2. WebSocker servers: Stateful, consistent hashing\n    - before a server removal, drain existing connections\n    - by marking the server to be in 'draining' state on LB\n    - so that no new conn will be routed to it\n    - auto-scaling stateful server is the job of a good LB\n3. User DB: sharding by user id\n4. Location cache: needs to handle 10 million concurrent users\n    - can shard cache server by id to handle 340k QPS\n    - need replicate to have HA\n5. Redis Pub/Sub server: (Identified as BottleNeck)\n    - Calc the number of pub/sub servers\n    - 1B * 10% = 100M users, each has a channel\n    - 400 average friends per user, 100 of them online\n    - 100M * 100 * 20bytes = 200GB memory\n    - 10M concurrent users, each has 40 online friends, over 30 seconds\n    - 10M * 40 / 30 = 13.33M updates per second\n    - Assume each redis server can push 100K updates to subscribers, system need 133 Redis servers\n    - Shard by ID, service discovery by etcd or zookeeper, keep a list of servers in the service \n        discovery component, and a simple API to update it. service discovery is a small kv store\n        for holding configuration data; clients(WebSocket servers) subscribe to any updates to the value(Redis servers)\n    - consistency hashing using hash ring\n    - over-provisioned cluster\n    - deal with mass resubscription events, location updates might be missed by clients and should be minimized\n    - when a pub/sub server goes down, the monitoring software should alert the on-call operator\n    - subscribe/unsubscribe callback on client app.\n    - users with large number of friends could cause hotspot issues - put a hard cap say 5000, p/b servers \n            will be scattered among many WebSocket servers, unlikely to cause hotspots\n    - Nearby random person - similar design, but maintain p/s servers for the area with geohash instead of friend list\n    - Erlang(programming language) is a great alternative to Redis P/S server, but hiring good Erlang programmers is hard\n    - Elixir, Erlang virtual machine BEAM, Erlang runtime OTP\n6. Use cases\n    - Add/remove friends\n    - Hotspots issues\n    - Extension: nearby random person",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "785KYTfd-LQ8Z7Z0iXurr",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4349.243960393048,
      "y": -135.33578392571627,
      "width": 888.079345703125,
      "height": 925,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b68",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1166772523,
      "version": 2015,
      "versionNonce": 831559650,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1727301921741,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Google maps\n\nStep 1 - Design scope\n1. Features to support: location update, navigation, ETA\n2. Road data: Terabytes of data obtained from third party \n3. Consider traffic conditions when navigate and ETA\n4. Support different travel mode - driving, walking, bus, bicycle, motorcycle\n5. Multi-stop directions\n6. Business places and photos (not required)\n7. Need to store historical location? (not in this problem)\n8. Replan may change route as traffic conditions change.\n\n* FR (map rendering)\nUpdate client location and locate on map\nUpdate road conditions based on client location update\nAuto plan routes given list of road segments, with ETA for each segment\n- input: option(walk/drive/bus), toll/free\n- road condition for each segment\nRender map given specific zoom level\n\n* NFR (accuracy, smooth map rendering, economic energy consumption)\nNear realtime, low latency\nCAP theorem: eventual consistency but Near realtime, say 500ms delay\nSystem needs to be HA\nSupport 1 billion DAU, suppose 20% use at the same time, amount to ~2500 QPS\n\n- High write volume. What's the R/W ratio?\n\n** Knowledge:\n- geocoding/reverse geocoding: convert an address to geo coordinate\n- map rendering with tiling, different sets of tiles in different zooming levels\n- road data: node/edges. representing the whole world in a single graph is resource wasting\n- map tiles vs routing tiles\n\n\n* Estimate\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Google maps\n\nStep 1 - Design scope\n1. Features to support: location update, navigation, ETA\n2. Road data: Terabytes of data obtained from third party \n3. Consider traffic conditions when navigate and ETA\n4. Support different travel mode - driving, walking, bus, bicycle, motorcycle\n5. Multi-stop directions\n6. Business places and photos (not required)\n7. Need to store historical location? (not in this problem)\n8. Replan may change route as traffic conditions change.\n\n* FR (map rendering)\nUpdate client location and locate on map\nUpdate road conditions based on client location update\nAuto plan routes given list of road segments, with ETA for each segment\n- input: option(walk/drive/bus), toll/free\n- road condition for each segment\nRender map given specific zoom level\n\n* NFR (accuracy, smooth map rendering, economic energy consumption)\nNear realtime, low latency\nCAP theorem: eventual consistency but Near realtime, say 500ms delay\nSystem needs to be HA\nSupport 1 billion DAU, suppose 20% use at the same time, amount to ~2500 QPS\n\n- High write volume. What's the R/W ratio?\n\n** Knowledge:\n- geocoding/reverse geocoding: convert an address to geo coordinate\n- map rendering with tiling, different sets of tiles in different zooming levels\n- road data: node/edges. representing the whole world in a single graph is resource wasting\n- map tiles vs routing tiles\n\n\n* Estimate\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HYlYg6stKZODUop9i7bem",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4337.031643716573,
      "y": 1102.3457628551287,
      "width": 968.2393798828125,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
